1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:20,699
This is the lawyer. It searches the patent databases around a gene, a molecule or a device. And it rules on whether a project may move or share data that a privacy law, an agreement or an ethics approval controls, always from the law, agreement or approval itself.

2
00:00:21,099 --> 00:00:34,799
It searches the patent databases worldwide for genes, proteins, molecules and devices, and explains the claims in plain words a scientist can act on: who owns what, what has expired, what is pending.

3
00:00:34,799 --> 00:00:54,500
For data, it reads the signed agreement or the ethics approval before it rules, and names what is missing when it cannot find one. It checks first whether there is anything to search, so a question with no patent at stake costs one step.

4
00:00:54,899 --> 00:01:07,799
It informs your decision. A binding opinion goes through the institution's research office, and the institution's patent office does the filing. The bookworm reads journal papers; the lawyer reads patent filings.

5
00:01:07,799 --> 00:01:15,900
For every patent, it says whether the status is confirmed across databases or only inferred.

6
00:01:16,300 --> 00:01:29,600
A real run, on a made-up question: may we publish a short protocol note and post our script under an open licence? That is a freedom-to-operate question: whether anyone's patent blocks you.

7
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:49,300
The verdict is Clear, after the first check alone. Publishing is not an act a patent reaches, so no database was searched, and the answer says so. It adds the one deadline to know: publishing starts the clock on filing a patent of your own.

8
00:01:49,700 --> 00:02:00,600
To use it, type to Claude Code and name the lawyer. Give it the molecule or the data, and what you intend to do with it.

9
00:02:00,600 --> 00:02:21,600
The first line is the verdict: Clear, Conflict or Unknown. The detail names what it searched, or the clause it relied on. It reads the agreements and ethics approvals your lab keeps in its project records. The adversary also sends it questions during an audit.
